  "id": "Compound::DB00934 - Gene::1565",
  "verbalization": "Maprotiline is linked to Cyp2d6 through a series of biological pathways that involve the enzyme Cyp2d6. The evidence suggests that Maprotiline causes Adenitis, which is then caused by either Protriptyline, Trimipramine, or Desipramine, all of which bind to Cyp2d6. The lowest common ancestors of these entities—Tricyclic Antidepressants and Organic Tricyclic Compounds—highlight a shared pharmacological profile, reinforcing the role of Cyp2d6 in metabolizing these compounds. The consistent presence of Cyp2d6 as a binding target across different pathways underscores a potential mechanism where Maprotiline indirectly influences Cyp2d6 activity through the activation of Tricyclic Antidepressants, which in turn bind to the enzyme. This suggests a broader role for Cyp2d6 in the metabolism of tricyclic antidepressants, with Maprotiline serving as a mediator in this process. The integration of these pathways highlights the enzyme's involvement in the metabolic pathway of tricyclic antidepressants, with Maprotiline acting as a causal link to the enzyme's activation.",
